================================================================================
                        SQL JOIN – COMPLETE GUIDE
================================================================================

WHAT IS JOIN IN SQL?
-------------------
-> A JOIN combines data from two or more tables based on a related column.
-> It retrieves related information stored across different tables.

Basic Example:

Students table                  Marks table
+-------------+------+          +-------------+-------+
| student_id  | name |          | student_id  | marks |
+-------------+------+          +-------------+-------+
| 1           | Ravi |          | 1           | 85    |
| 2           | Priya|          | 2           | 90    |
+-------------+------+          +-------------+-------+

After JOIN:
+-------------+------+-------+
| student_id  | name | marks |
+-------------+------+-------+
| 1           | Ravi | 85    |
| 2           | Priya| 90    |
+-------------+------+-------+

================================================================================
TYPES OF JOINS (6 TYPES)
================================================================================

1. INNER JOIN
2. LEFT JOIN
3. RIGHT JOIN
4. FULL JOIN (FULL OUTER JOIN)
5. CROSS JOIN
6. SELF JOIN

================================================================================
1. INNER JOIN
================================================================================

Definition: Returns only the matching rows from both tables.

Syntax:
  SELECT *
  FROM table1
  INNER JOIN table2
  ON table1.id = table2.id;

Example Tables:

Students                        Marks
+----+-------+                  +----+-------+
| id | name  |                  | id | marks |
+----+-------+                  +----+-------+
| 1  | Ravi  |                  | 1  | 85    |
| 2  | Priya |                  | 2  | 90    |
| 3  | Arun  |                  | 4  | 70    |
+----+-------+                  +----+-------+

Query:
  SELECT Students.id, Students.name, Marks.marks
  FROM Students
  INNER JOIN Marks
  ON Students.id = Marks.id;

Output:
+----+-------+-------+
| id | name  | marks |
+----+-------+-------+
| 1  | Ravi  | 85    |
| 2  | Priya | 90    |
+----+-------+-------+

Note: Only matching IDs (1,2) are returned. Arun (id3) and marks for id4 are excluded.

INNER JOIN – Order of Execution (Logical Steps):
  Step 1: Take first row from left table -> (1, Ravi)
  Step 2: Search matching row in right table -> (1, 85)
  Step 3: Condition 1=1 -> TRUE
  Step 4: Combine and return -> (1, Ravi, 85)
  Step 5: Repeat for all rows
  Step 6: Rows without matches are ignored

================================================================================
2. LEFT JOIN
================================================================================

Definition: Returns all rows from the left table + matching rows from the right
            table. If no match, right columns are NULL.

Syntax:
  SELECT *
  FROM table1
  LEFT JOIN table2
  ON table1.id = table2.id;

Query (using same Students & Marks tables):
  SELECT Students.id, Students.name, Marks.marks
  FROM Students
  LEFT JOIN Marks
  ON Students.id = Marks.id;

Output:
+----+-------+-------+
| id | name  | marks |
+----+-------+-------+
| 1  | Ravi  | 85    |
| 2  | Priya | 90    |
| 3  | Arun  | NULL  |
+----+-------+-------+

LEFT JOIN – Order of Execution:
  Step 1: Take every row from left table (Ravi, Priya, Arun)
  Step 2: Find matching rows in right table
  Step 3: If match exists -> combine rows
  Step 4: If no match -> return NULL for right table columns
  Step 5: Return all left table rows

================================================================================
3. RIGHT JOIN
================================================================================

Definition: Returns all rows from the right table + matching rows from the left
            table. If no match, left columns are NULL.

Syntax:
  SELECT *
  FROM table1
  RIGHT JOIN table2
  ON table1.id = table2.id;

Query:
  SELECT Students.id, Students.name, Marks.marks
  FROM Students
  RIGHT JOIN Marks
  ON Students.id = Marks.id;

Output:
+------+-------+-------+
| id   | name  | marks |
+------+-------+-------+
| 1    | Ravi  | 85    |
| 2    | Priya | 90    |
| NULL | NULL  | 70    |
+------+-------+-------+

RIGHT JOIN – Order of Execution:
  Step 1: Take every row from right table
  Step 2: Search matching rows in left table
  Step 3: If match exists -> combine rows
  Step 4: If no match -> return NULL for left table columns
  Step 5: Return all right table rows

================================================================================
4. FULL JOIN (FULL OUTER JOIN)
================================================================================

Definition: Returns all rows from both tables. Matching rows are combined;
            missing sides get NULL.

Syntax:
  SELECT *
  FROM table1
  FULL OUTER JOIN table2
  ON table1.id = table2.id;

Output (using same tables):
+------+-------+-------+
| id   | name  | marks |
+------+-------+-------+
| 1    | Ravi  | 85    |
| 2    | Priya | 90    |
| 3    | Arun  | NULL  |
| NULL | NULL  | 70    |
+------+-------+-------+

FULL JOIN – Order of Execution:
  Step 1: Perform INNER JOIN first -> (1,Ravi,85), (2,Priya,90)
  Step 2: Add non-matching rows from left table -> (3,Arun,NULL)
  Step 3: Add non-matching rows from right table -> (NULL,NULL,70)
  Step 4: Combine and return all rows

================================================================================
5. CROSS JOIN
================================================================================

Definition: Returns the Cartesian product – every combination of rows from both
            tables. No ON clause.

Syntax:
  SELECT *
  FROM table1
  CROSS JOIN table2;

Example:

Table A         Table B
+----+          +--------+
| id |          | letter |
+----+          +--------+
| 1  |          | A      |
| 2  |          | B      |
+----+          +--------+

Output:
+----+--------+
| id | letter |
+----+--------+
| 1  | A      |
| 1  | B      |
| 2  | A      |
| 2  | B      |
+----+--------+

CROSS JOIN – Order of Execution:
  Step 1: Take first row from first table (1)
  Step 2: Combine with ALL rows from second table -> (1,A), (1,B)
  Step 3: Take second row from first table (2)
  Step 4: Combine with ALL rows from second table -> (2,A), (2,B)
  Step 5: Return every possible combination

================================================================================
6. SELF JOIN
================================================================================

Definition: A table joins with itself. Used when rows within the same table are
            related (e.g., employee–manager).

Syntax:
  SELECT *
  FROM table1 AS alias1
  JOIN table1 AS alias2
  ON alias1.col = alias2.col;

Example – Employee Table:
+--------+----------+------------+
| emp_id | emp_name | manager_id |
+--------+----------+------------+
| 1      | Ravi     | NULL       |
| 2      | Arun     | 1          |
+--------+----------+------------+

Query:
  SELECT e.emp_name AS Employee,
         m.emp_name AS Manager
  FROM Employee e
  LEFT JOIN Employee m
  ON e.manager_id = m.emp_id;

Output:
+----------+---------+
| Employee | Manager |
+----------+---------+
| Ravi     | NULL    |
| Arun     | Ravi    |
+----------+---------+

SELF JOIN – Order of Execution:
  Step 1: Create two copies of the same table: e and m
  Step 2: Take row from first copy: Arun, manager_id = 1
  Step 3: Find matching emp_id in second copy: Ravi, emp_id = 1
  Step 4: Condition 1 = 1 -> TRUE
  Step 5: Combine rows: Arun -> Ravi

================================================================================
SQL QUERY EXECUTION ORDER (Logical)
================================================================================

Order  Clause      Description
------------------------------------------------------------
1      FROM        Identify source table(s)
2      JOIN        Combine with other tables
3      ON          Apply join conditions
4      WHERE       Filter rows
5      GROUP BY    Aggregate groups
6      HAVING      Filter groups
7      SELECT      Choose columns / expressions
8      ORDER BY    Sort results
9      LIMIT       Restrict number of rows

Example Execution Walkthrough:

Query:
  SELECT name, marks
  FROM Students
  INNER JOIN Marks ON Students.id = Marks.id
  WHERE marks > 80
  ORDER BY marks DESC;

Execution Flow:
  1. FROM -> take Students table
  2. JOIN -> combine with Marks table
  3. ON -> match rows on id
  4. WHERE -> keep rows where marks > 80
  5. SELECT -> pick name and marks columns
  6. ORDER BY -> sort by marks descending

================================================================================
QUICK REVISION TABLE
================================================================================

JOIN TYPE      | RETURNS
---------------+------------------------------------------------
INNER JOIN     | Only matching rows from both tables
LEFT JOIN      | All rows from left + matched rows from right (NULL if none)
RIGHT JOIN     | All rows from right + matched rows from left (NULL if none)
FULL JOIN      | All rows from both tables (NULL on missing side)
CROSS JOIN     | Every combination of rows (Cartesian product)
SELF JOIN      | Table joined with itself (uses aliases)

================================================================================
IMPORTANT INTERVIEW POINTS
================================================================================

1. JOIN combines data from multiple tables using a related column.
2. The ON clause defines the matching condition.
3. INNER JOIN returns only common data.
4. LEFT JOIN preserves all rows from the left table.
5. RIGHT JOIN preserves all rows from the right table.
6. FULL JOIN preserves all rows from both sides.
7. CROSS JOIN generates a Cartesian product (use with care).
8. SELF JOIN joins a table to itself – requires table aliases.
9. SQL logically executes FROM and JOIN before SELECT.
10. In LEFT JOIN, filtering on the right table in WHERE can turn it into an
    INNER JOIN – place such filters in the ON clause if you want to keep
    non‑matching left rows.
